The treesitter highlighter can lose conceal_lines marks after an edit.

A buffer edit clears all conceal_lines marks and the _conceal_checked
flags. When the next parse runs asynchronously (it takes more than 3
ms), a redraw happens with `parsing == true`. That redraw iterates
stale highlight states, so it adds no conceal_lines marks back. Still,
on_range_impl marks every visible row as checked. The parse completion
callback invalidates only the rows in the tree diff. A row outside the
diff keeps checked == true with no mark, and its line never conceals
again until the next edit.

The functional test "treesitter highlighting (markdown) works with
concealed lines" (highlight_spec.lua:1462) fails this way on slow CI
machines. To reproduce on a fast machine, set default_parse_timeout_ns
to 1 in languagetree.lua; the test then fails on each run.

The fix: do not mark rows as checked while `parsing` is true. The full
redraw after the parse completes checks them again and adds the marks.

Drop this patch when upstream neovim fixes the race.

--- a/runtime/lua/vim/treesitter/highlighter.lua
+++ b/runtime/lua/vim/treesitter/highlighter.lua
@@ -357,8 +357,13 @@
       range_end_col = 0
     end
   end
-  for i = range_start_row, range_end_row - 1 do
-    self._conceal_checked[i] = self._conceal_line or nil
+  -- A redraw during an incomplete async parse iterates stale highlight
+  -- states and cannot add conceal_lines marks. Do not mark rows checked;
+  -- the full redraw after the parse completes checks them again.
+  if not self.parsing then
+    for i = range_start_row, range_end_row - 1 do
+      self._conceal_checked[i] = self._conceal_line or nil
+    end
   end
 
   local MAX_ROW = 2147483647 -- sentinel for skipping to the end of file
